// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_SLICE_ROLE_UNSATISFIED
//
// Plan 284 F.1/F.2a neg: `Weird` declares `@index(r Range)` and does NOT
// declare `@end_index()`, so it does not satisfy the slice role `RangeIndex`
// (D470) and `w[2..]` must be REFUSED — by a named diagnostic, not an ICE and
// not a panic.
//
// `nova check` accepts the form on its own: `infer_expr_type`'s `ExprKind::Index`
// arm returns `obj_tr` unconditionally for any Range index, without verifying
// that an `@index(Range)` impl exists at all. So the refusal is codegen's, and
// this fixture pins that it stays a diagnostic.
//
// The file used to expect `E_OPEN_RANGE_NO_LEN` and explained the refusal by a
// missing field literally named `len` (the type has only `cap`). That was the
// retired test: a field named `len` admitted any type whose `len` is a counter
// or a capacity. Ironically `Weird`'s own field is `cap` — under the role the
// field name stops mattering entirely, and what is missing is a METHOD.

module neg.openrange_no_len_neg
import std.collections.range.{Range}

type Weird {
    cap int
}

fn Weird @index(r Range) -> Weird {
    Weird { cap: r.end - r.start }
}

fn main() -> () {
    ro w = Weird { cap: 5 }
    // NO `nova:expect` PIN YET, and that is measured, not forgotten. The
    // diagnostic fires exactly as intended -- verified 2026-09-07:
    //   [E_SLICE_ROLE_UNSATISFIED] type `Nova_Weird*` does not support range
    //   slicing (`[a..]`/`[a..b]`): it does not satisfy the slice role ...
    // but it carries no span, so a pin cannot match it and the runner refuses
    // honestly: "pinned to line 33, but the matching error landed on line ?".
    //
    // What closes this, with the precedent that decides how: plan 280 met the
    // same wall and moved the refusal INTO THE CHECKER, where spans exist
    // (`compiler-codegen/src/types/mod.rs:6636-6641`, verbatim: "a codegen-side
    // error carries no span and the neg fixture reported 'landed on line ?' --",
    // "a diagnostic that points nowhere is the very class this plan exists to
    // remove"). The checker is also where this check belongs for its own
    // reason, stated in the header above: `nova check` accepts the form today
    // because the Index arm returns `obj_tr` for any Range index without
    // verifying an impl exists. One change closes both, and the pin comes back
    // WITH it, as its proof (plan 284, F.2a).
    ro v = w[2..]
    ro _ = v
}
